ased their unique compositional method, involving extensive improvisation sessions from which appealing segments were later cut and potentially enhanced with arrangements. The first vinyl focused on rhythm, concluding with the sublime 18-minute funk trance of \"Halleluwah,\" which nearly anticipated drum and bass, while the second vinyl explored more abstract and cosmic themes with long tracks like \"Aumgn\" and \"Peking O,\" revisiting the atonal spirit of Stockhausen. It is widely regarded as the band's most famous and acclaimed work, frequently appearing on lists of the greatest rock albums in history, offering a wilder, darker, and tenser form of psychedelia in contrast to Pink Floyd's meticulously constructed progressive approach, with its groovy rhythms and often dark sonic pollution serving as a clear reference for John Lydon's band PiL. \"Este tal vez sea el trabajo más famoso y destacado de la banda, el cual aparece en todas las listas de los mejores discos de rock de la historia.\"","year":1971,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Central Sonora","episode":"CAN ｜ La Banda que (posiblemente) Inspiró a tu banda preferida","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4qHiw2wHWAI"}]}],"book_tracks":[{"id":219651823668291,"artist":"Can","artist_id":126657727149758,"title":"Tago Mago","year":1971,"ess":1,"yt":"V8xPreBt1U4","kind":"a","book":{"id":8,"title":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/"},"scene":"Kosmische Musik y krautrock","scene_id":107234531659708,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/#kosmische","chapters":["Sección 3.7"],"pages":[[1,66,null]]},{"id":219651823668291,"artist":"Can","artist_id":126657727149758,"title":"Tago Mago","year":1971,"ess":1,"yt":"V8xPreBt1U4","kind":null,"book":{"id":10,"title":"Loops 1 y 2. Una historia de la música electrónica","author":"Javier Blánquez (ed.)","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/loops_timeline/"},"scene":"Krautrock","scene_id":44516675266